//! The looks gallery ("Moods"): one-click desktop looks that set the bar,
//! desktop cards / visualizers, a palette, typography and the radius /
//! opacity scale together — the built-in [`LOOKS`] and your own
//! ([`crate::user_looks`], children of a built-in one).
//!
//! A look never ships assets: its palette is generated by Mandara's HCT
//! engine from an OKLCH seed ([`LookDef::seed`]), and applying it never
//! touches the wallpaper image. With [`LookOptions::wallpaper_colors`] the
//! palette is instead taken from the *current* wallpaper using the look's
//! scheme variant.
//!
//! # What a Look owns, and what stays yours
//!
//! A Look owns exactly what [`LookDef::apply`] writes:
//!
//! * the palette (`theme.source`, `theme.mode`, `theme.adjustments`);
//! * the bar: its layout, edge, frame and opacity (a plain bar), or a whole
//!   bar style preset with its height and rail; the bar items **it** lays
//!   out ([`crate::bar_items`]);
//! * the interface and display fonts, the radius scale and (unless
//!   `looks.keep_components`) the component designs;
//! * the desktop widgets **it** adds, recorded in [`LooksState::widgets`];
//! * the settings it names on top (dock, motion feel, launcher look,
//!   notification corner, bar auto-hide, hot corners), recorded in
//!   [`LooksState::settings`] so the next look puts back what it changed;
//! * the plugins it switched on or off (recorded, undone the same way).
//!
//! Everything else is yours and no look touches it: the wallpaper image,
//! the desktop widgets you added or changed (ids, places, settings), the
//! bar items you added (on the bar, the rails and the side bars), the
//! plugins you switched on, the bar's size unless the look carries a bar
//! style, and every other setting. A look's widget you changed becomes
//! yours: the next look leaves it where you put it.
//!
//! Applying a look from your own setup first saves that setup as the
//! [`BEFORE_PROFILE`] look profile (exactly [`SNAPSHOT_PATHS`]), so
//! "Restore my look" — or Studio's profile undo — brings it back. Switching
//! between looks keeps that first snapshot. Restoring keeps your own
//! widgets and bar items, including the ones you added while a look was on.

